B="https://havardjarl.github.io/aquarium-calculator/"
AMAZON_TAG=""  # set e.g. yourtag-20 when Associates is ready
NAV='<header><a href="index.html"><b>Aquarium Calculator</b></a> · <a href="guides.html">Guides</a> · <a href="about.html">About</a></header>'
AD=lambda s:f'<div class="ad" data-slot="{s}"><!-- AD SLOT ({s}): paste ad unit here --></div>'
AFF=lambda t:f'<div class="aff"><!-- AFFILIATE SLOT: set AMAZON_TAG --><p>{t}</p></div>'
FOOT='<footer><p>As an Amazon Associate this site may earn from qualifying purchases. Figures are estimates; always check your equipment manufacturer\'s guidance. Last updated October 2026.</p><p><a href="about.html">About, privacy &amp; affiliate disclosure</a> · <a href="guides.html">All guides</a></p></footer>'
def page(fn,title,desc,body,h1,faq=None):
    url=B+("" if fn=="index.html" else fn)
    ld='{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","name":"%s","url":"%s"}'%(title,url)
    if faq:
        import json
        ld=json.dumps({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":q,"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":a}} for q,a in faq]})
        body+="<h2>FAQ</h2>"+"".join(f"<h3>{q}</h3><p>{a}</p>" for q,a in faq)
    open(fn,"w").write(f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>{title}</title><meta name="description" content="{desc}"><link rel="canonical" href="{url}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{title}"><meta property="og:description" content="{desc}"><meta property="og:url" content="{url}"><meta property="og:type" content="website">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><script type="application/ld+json">{ld}</script></head><body>{NAV}<main>{AD("top")}<h1>{h1}</h1>{body}{AD("bottom")}</main>{FOOT}</body></html>''')
    return fn
TOOL=open("tool.html").read()
pages=[]
pages.append(page("index.html","Aquarium Calculator: Volume, Gallons, Substrate & Heater Size","Free aquarium calculator: enter tank dimensions to get gallons and liters, filled weight, how much gravel or sand to buy, heater wattage and filter flow.",TOOL+'''
<h2>How to use it</h2><p>Measure the <b>inside</b> of the tank (glass is usually 1/4–1/2 in thick, so outside dimensions overstate volume). Enter length, width and height, choose your units, and the calculator shows gross volume, a realistic water volume (after substrate and the gap below the rim), the total filled weight, how much substrate to buy, and a starting point for heater and filter size.</p>
'''+AFF("Shopping for substrate or a heater?")+'''<p>Read more: <a href="tank-volume.html">how tank volume is calculated</a>, <a href="substrate-guide.html">how much substrate</a>, <a href="heater-size.html">heater size</a>.</p>''',"Aquarium Calculator",
[("How do I calculate aquarium gallons?","Multiply inside length × width × height in inches and divide by 231 to get US gallons. In centimeters, divide by 1000 to get liters."),
 ("Why is my actual water volume less than the label?","Labels are usually nominal. Glass thickness, substrate, decor and the gap below the rim typically reduce the real water volume by roughly 10–20%.")]))
P=[("tank-volume.html","Aquarium Volume Formula: Rectangular, Cylinder, Bow-Front & Hexagon","How to calculate aquarium volume in gallons and liters for rectangular, cylinder, bow-front, corner and hexagon tanks, with worked examples.","How to Calculate Aquarium Volume",'''<p>Volume is the space inside the glass. Always use inside dimensions.</p>
<h2>Rectangular</h2><p>L × W × H (inches) ÷ 231 = US gallons. Example: 24 × 12 × 16 in = 4,608 in³ ÷ 231 ≈ 19.9 gal (≈ 75.5 L). In cm: L × W × H ÷ 1000 = liters.</p>
<h2>Cylinder</h2><p>π × r² × H. A 12 in diameter, 24 in tall cylinder: 3.1416 × 36 × 24 ≈ 2,714 in³ ≈ 11.8 gal.</p>
<h2>Bow-front</h2><p>Treat it as a rectangle plus a half ellipse: area ≈ L × D_back + (π/4) × L × (D_front − D_back), where D_back is the depth at the ends and D_front the depth at the center of the bow. Multiply by H, then ÷ 231.</p>
<h2>Hexagon</h2><p>Area = 2.598 × side² (regular hexagon), then × H.</p>
<h2>Units</h2><p>1 US gallon = 3.785 L = 231 in³. 1 UK (imperial) gallon = 4.546 L. Many UK and European tank sizes are quoted in liters.</p>'''),
("substrate-guide.html","How Much Gravel or Sand for an Aquarium? Depth & Weight Guide","How much aquarium gravel, sand or aquasoil to buy: recommended depth by tank type, the formula, and pounds for common tank sizes.","How Much Substrate Does Your Aquarium Need?",'''<p>Substrate depends on the <b>footprint</b> (length × width), not gallons. A tall 29 gal and a 20 gal long can need similar amounts.</p>
<h2>Formula</h2><p>L × W × depth (in) = in³. Divide by 1,728 for cubic feet. Typical gravel and sand weigh roughly 90–110 lb per cubic foot (approximate bulk density; check the bag). Lightweight planted aquasoils are much lighter, often around half that.</p>
<h2>Typical depths (common hobby guidance)</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Depth</th></tr><tr><td>Fish-only, gravel</td><td>1–2 in</td></tr><tr><td>Sand for bottom sifters</td><td>1–1.5 in</td></tr><tr><td>Planted</td><td>2–3 in, often sloped deeper at the back</td></tr></table>
<h2>Examples at 2 in depth, ~100 lb/ft³</h2><table><tr><th>Tank (footprint)</th><th>Approx. lb</th></tr><tr><td>10 gal (20×10)</td><td>~23</td></tr><tr><td>20 long (30×12)</td><td>~42</td></tr><tr><td>29 gal (30×12)</td><td>~42</td></tr><tr><td>40 breeder (36×18)</td><td>~75</td></tr><tr><td>55 gal (48×13)</td><td>~72</td></tr><tr><td>75 gal (48×18)</td><td>~100</td></tr></table><p>These are calculated estimates. Use the <a href="index.html">calculator</a> for your exact tank.</p>'''),
("heater-size.html","What Size Aquarium Heater Do I Need? Watts per Gallon Guide","Aquarium heater sizing: the 3–5 watts per gallon rule, how room temperature changes it, and when to use two heaters.","What Size Aquarium Heater Do You Need?",'''<p>A common rule of thumb is <b>3–5 watts per US gallon</b> (roughly 1 W per liter). Use the high end when the room is much colder than the target temperature.</p>
<h2>Rule by temperature difference</h2><table><tr><th>Target minus room temp</th><th>Watts per gallon (rule of thumb)</th></tr><tr><td>Up to ~5 °F (3 °C)</td><td>~2.5–3</td></tr><tr><td>~10 °F (5–6 °C)</td><td>~3–4</td></tr><tr><td>~15 °F+ (8 °C+)</td><td>~5</td></tr></table>
<h2>Two heaters for large tanks</h2><p>For tanks above about 50–75 gallons many keepers split the wattage across two heaters at opposite ends: better heat distribution, and if one sticks on it is less likely to overheat the tank. A separate temperature controller adds another safety layer.</p><p>Always follow the heater manufacturer's rated tank size.</p>'''),
("tank-weight.html","How Much Does a Fish Tank Weigh When Full? Weight Chart","Filled aquarium weight: water weighs about 8.34 lb per US gallon. Estimated full weights for 10–125 gallon tanks and floor considerations.","How Much Does a Full Aquarium Weigh?",'''<p>Fresh water weighs about <b>8.34 lb per US gallon</b> (1 kg per liter); saltwater about 8.5–8.6 lb/gal. Add glass, substrate, rock and stand.</p>
<h2>Water-only weight (nominal gallons)</h2><table><tr><th>Tank</th><th>Water</th></tr><tr><td>10 gal</td><td>83 lb</td></tr><tr><td>20 gal</td><td>167 lb</td></tr><tr><td>29 gal</td><td>242 lb</td></tr><tr><td>40 gal</td><td>334 lb</td></tr><tr><td>55 gal</td><td>459 lb</td></tr><tr><td>75 gal</td><td>626 lb</td></tr><tr><td>125 gal</td><td>1,043 lb</td></tr></table><p>A rough planning figure people use for the total setup is ~10 lb per gallon or more. For large tanks on upper floors, consult someone qualified about your floor; this site cannot assess structural loads.</p>'''),
("filter-flow-rate.html","Aquarium Filter Flow Rate (GPH) Calculator Guide","How many gallons per hour your aquarium filter should turn over: the 4–10x rule, rated vs real flow, and setup-specific tips.","Aquarium Filter Flow Rate: How Many GPH?",'''<p>Common guidance: the filter's rated flow should turn over the tank volume about <b>4–6 times per hour</b> for most freshwater community tanks, more for messy fish (goldfish, cichlids) and less for slow-water species like bettas.</p>
<p>Example: a 40 gallon tank × 5 = ~200 GPH rated flow.</p><h2>Rated vs real flow</h2><p>Manufacturer figures are usually measured without media or head height; real flow is lower once the filter is loaded. That is why many keepers size up.</p>'''),
("water-change.html","Aquarium Water Change Calculator: How Much and How Often","How to calculate a percentage water change in gallons, how water changes dilute nitrate, and how to match temperature and dechlorinate.","Water Change Calculator & Guide",'''<label>Tank water volume (gal) <input id="wv" type="number" value="20"></label><label>Change (%) <input id="wp" type="number" value="25"></label><button type="button" onclick="document.getElementById('wo').textContent=(wv.value*wp.value/100).toFixed(1)+' gallons ('+(wv.value*wp.value/100*3.785).toFixed(1)+' L) to remove and replace'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="wo"></div>
<h2>Dilution</h2><p>Each change lowers dissolved nitrate by roughly the same percentage (if the new water has none). A 25% change takes 40 ppm to about 30 ppm; two 25% changes reach about 22.5 ppm.</p><h2>Tips</h2><p>Use a dechlorinator dosed for the full tank volume when using tap water with chloramine, and match the temperature.</p>'''),
("tank-sizes.html","Standard Aquarium Sizes Chart: Dimensions, Gallons & Liters","Common US aquarium sizes with typical dimensions in inches and cm, volume in gallons and liters and footprint. Dimensions vary by brand.","Standard Aquarium Sizes & Dimensions",'''<p>Typical dimensions for common glass tanks. Brands differ by up to an inch, so measure yours.</p><table><tr><th>Tank</th><th>L×W×H (in)</th><th>Liters (nominal)</th></tr>
<tr><td>5 gal</td><td>16×8×10</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>10 gal</td><td>20×10×12</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>20 high</td><td>24×12×16</td><td>76</td></tr><tr><td>20 long</td><td>30×12×12</td><td>76</td></tr><tr><td>29 gal</td><td>30×12×18</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>40 breeder</td><td>36×18×16</td><td>151</td></tr><tr><td>55 gal</td><td>48×13×21</td><td>208</td></tr><tr><td>75 gal</td><td>48×18×21</td><td>284</td></tr><tr><td>125 gal</td><td>72×18×21</td><td>473</td></tr></table><p>Note how the 20 long and the 29 have the same footprint; that matters for substrate and for bottom-dwelling fish.</p>'''),
("gallons-to-liters.html","Aquarium Gallons to Liters Converter (US & UK)","Convert aquarium volume between US gallons, UK gallons and liters, with a quick conversion table.","Gallons to Liters for Aquariums",'''<label>Value <input id="gv" type="number" value="20"></label><button type="button" onclick="gl.textContent=gv.value+' US gal = '+(gv.value*3.78541).toFixed(1)+' L | '+gv.value+' L = '+(gv.value/3.78541).toFixed(2)+' US gal | '+gv.value+' UK gal = '+(gv.value*4.54609).toFixed(1)+' L'">Convert</button><div class="out" id="gl"></div><p>1 US gal = 3.785 L; 1 UK gal = 4.546 L; 1 L = 0.264 US gal.</p>'''),
("cycling-guide.html","How to Cycle a Fish Tank: Fishless Cycling Step by Step","A practical fishless cycling guide: ammonia source, what to test, the typical timeline and when it is safe to add fish.","How to Cycle a New Aquarium (Fishless)",'''<p>Cycling grows bacteria that convert toxic ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate. It commonly takes several weeks; there is no fixed timeline.</p><ol><li>Set up the tank, filter and heater; dechlorinate the water.</li><li>Add an ammonia source (pure bottled ammonia or fish food) to about 2 ppm.</li><li>Test ammonia, nitrite and nitrate every few days with a liquid test kit.</li><li>Ammonia falls, nitrite rises, then nitrite falls and nitrate rises.</li><li>It is cycled when 2 ppm ammonia clears to 0 ammonia and 0 nitrite within 24 hours. Do a large water change, then add fish gradually.</li></ol>'''+AFF("A liquid test kit is the one essential purchase:")),
("stocking-guide.html","Aquarium Stocking Guide: Inch-per-Gallon & Better Rules","How many fish for your tank: why inch-per-gallon is flawed, bioload rules of thumb, and safer stocking for common community sizes.","How Many Fish for Your Aquarium?",'''<p>The old <b>one inch of fish per gallon</b> rule is a rough starting point for slender freshwater community fish in a filtered, cycled tank. It fails for bulky fish (goldfish, cichlids), messy eaters, and tiny nano tanks where surface area and filtration matter more than gallons alone.</p>
<h2>Better heuristics</h2><ul><li>Match adult size, not store juvenile size.</li><li>Prioritize footprint and swimming length for active species.</li><li>Stock slowly after a full fishless cycle; add a few fish every 1-2 weeks while testing ammonia and nitrite.</li><li>Overfilter for goldfish and large cichlids; under-stock for bettas and labyrinth fish that prefer calm water.</li></ul>
<h2>Rough community examples (filtered, freshwater)</h2><table><tr><th>Tank</th><th>Conservative start</th></tr><tr><td>10 gal</td><td>A nano community (e.g. small tetras) or one betta; skip goldfish</td></tr><tr><td>20 long</td><td>Small school + bottom dwellers</td></tr><tr><td>40 breeder</td><td>Larger school or peaceful centerpiece fish</td></tr><tr><td>75 gal+</td><td>Room for bigger species or mixed community</td></tr></table><p>Use the <a href="index.html">volume calculator</a> for real water volume after substrate, then stock for <b>adult</b> needs.</p>'''),
("led-lighting.html","Aquarium LED Lighting: Watts & PAR for Planted Tanks","How much light your aquarium needs: watts-per-gallon myths, PAR ranges for low/medium/high tech planted tanks, and photoperiod tips.","Aquarium Lighting for Plants & Fish",'''<p>Watts-per-gallon was useful for old T5/T8 fluorescents. With LEDs, <b>spectrum and PAR</b> matter more than watt stickers.</p>
<h2>Planted tank PAR (rough targets at substrate)</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>PAR</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td>Low tech / easy plants</td><td>~15-30</td><td>Anubias, java fern, crypts</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>~30-50</td><td>Stem plants with decent ferts</td></tr><tr><td>High tech / CO2</td><td>~50-100+</td><td>Needs CO2 and fert dosing</td></tr></table>
<h2>Photoperiod</h2><p>Most tanks do well on about 6-8 hours. Longer lights without enough plants or CO2 often mean algae. Raise intensity slowly on a new scape.</p><p>Fish-only tanks need far less light; prioritize a timer and avoid window sun that overheats the water.</p>'''),
("nitrate-calculator.html","Aquarium Nitrate Dilution Calculator","Estimate how water changes lower nitrate: enter current ppm and change percent to see the new level.","Nitrate After a Water Change",'''<label>Current nitrate (ppm) <input id="n0" type="number" value="40"></label><label>Water change (%) <input id="np" type="number" value="25"></label><label>New water nitrate (ppm) <input id="nn" type="number" value="0"></label><button type="button" onclick="var a=+n0.value,p=+np.value/100,b=+nn.value;no.textContent='After one change: '+((a*(1-p)+b*p).toFixed(1))+' ppm. After two identical changes: '+((((a*(1-p)+b*p)*(1-p)+b*p)).toFixed(1))+' ppm.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="no"></div>
<h2>Why it matters</h2><p>Water changes dilute nitrate roughly by the percentage you replace (if tap water is near 0 ppm nitrate). They do not remove the cause: feeding, bioload and plant uptake still set how fast nitrate climbs between changes.</p><p>See also the <a href="water-change.html">water change volume guide</a> and <a href="cycling-guide.html">cycling guide</a>.</p>'''),
('betta-tank-size.html','Betta Tank Size Guide: Minimum Gallons & Dimensions','How big a tank a betta needs: why bowls fail, recommended gallons, footprint, and heater/filter tips for a single betta.','How Big a Tank Does a Betta Need?','<p>A healthy adult betta does best in at least <b>5 US gallons</b> (about 19 L). Many keepers prefer 10 gallons for stable temperature and water quality. Bowls and tiny cups swing temperature and ammonia too fast.</p>\n<h2>Why gallons matter for bettas</h2><p>Bettas are labyrinth fish and can breathe air, but they still need space to swim, a heater for tropical temperatures (about 76-80 F / 24-27 C), and a gentle filter. Small volumes crash after a missed water change.</p>\n<h2>Practical setup</h2><table><tr><th>Volume</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td>Under 5 gal</td><td>Harder to keep stable; avoid for long-term homes</td></tr><tr><td>5 gal</td><td>Common minimum with heater, soft flow, weekly changes</td></tr><tr><td>10 gal+</td><td>More forgiving; room for plants and hideouts</td></tr></table>\n<p>Measure inside dimensions with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>, then size a low-flow filter (or baffle a HOB) so the current is calm. See also the <a href="heater-size.html">heater size guide</a> and <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a>.</p>'),
('ammonia-calculator.html','Aquarium Ammonia Dilution Calculator','Estimate how a water change lowers ammonia ppm: enter current level and change percent.','Ammonia After a Water Change','<label>Current ammonia (ppm) <input id="a0" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label><label>Water change (%) <input id="ap" type="number" value="50"></label><label>New water ammonia (ppm) <input id="an" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var a=+a0.value,p=+ap.value/100,b=+an.value;ao.textContent=\'After one change: \'+((a*(1-p)+b*p).toFixed(2))+\' ppm. After two identical changes: \'+((((a*(1-p)+b*p)*(1-p)+b*p)).toFixed(2))+\' ppm.\'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="ao"></div>\n<h2>Emergency dilution</h2><p>Ammonia and nitrite should stay near 0 in a cycled tank. If you measure a spike, a large water change (often 50%+) with dechlorinated, temperature-matched water is the usual first step while you find the cause (overfeeding, filter crash, uncycled tank).</p>\n<p>This tool only models dilution. It does not replace a liquid test kit or manufacturer guidance. Pair it with the <a href="cycling-guide.html">fishless cycling guide</a> and <a href="nitrate-calculator.html">nitrate calculator</a>.</p>'),
('aquarium-salt-calculator.html','Aquarium Salt Dosage Calculator (tsp & cups)','Dose aquarium salt by tank gallons: common 1 tbsp per 5 gal guidance, with tsp and gram estimates.','Aquarium Salt Dosage by Gallons','<label>Tank water volume (US gal) <input id="sv" type="number" value="20" step="any"></label><label>Dose level <select id="sd"><option value="1">Light (~1 tbsp / 5 gal)</option><option value="2">Moderate (~1 tbsp / 3 gal)</option><option value="0.5">Very light (~0.5 tbsp / 5 gal)</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var g=+sv.value,m=+sd.value;var tbsp=g/5*m;var tsp=tbsp*3;var grms=tbsp*17.5;so.textContent=g+\' gal -> about \'+tbsp.toFixed(1)+\' tbsp (\'+tsp.toFixed(1)+\' tsp, ~\'+grms.toFixed(0)+\' g) of aquarium salt. Dissolve in tank water before adding.\'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="so"></div>\n<h2>Important caveats</h2><p>Aquarium salt (sodium chloride without additives) is used by some keepers for certain freshwater treatments. Many plants, scaleless fish, and invertebrates dislike salt. Never use table salt with iodine or anti-caking agents unless a vet or product label says otherwise.</p>\n<p>Always follow a medication or veterinary protocol when treating disease. Use real water volume from the <a href="index.html">volume calculator</a> (after substrate), not the tank\'s nominal label.</p>'),
('quarantine-tank-size.html','Quarantine Tank Size Guide for New Fish','What size quarantine tank to use: common 10-20 gallon setups, bare-bottom tips, and how long to quarantine.','Quarantine Tank Size for New Fish','<p>A dedicated quarantine (QT) tank lets you watch new fish for disease before they join the display. A common starter QT is <b>10-20 US gallons</b> with a heater, gentle filter, and bare bottom for easy cleaning.</p>\n<h2>Sizing rules of thumb</h2><table><tr><th>Fish</th><th>Typical QT</th></tr><tr><td>Nano / small community</td><td>10 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Medium community / few adults</td><td>20 long</td></tr><tr><td>Large or aggressive species</td><td>Match adult needs; do not undersize</td></tr></table>\n<h2>How long</h2><p>Many hobbyists quarantine about 2-4 weeks while watching for ich, velvet, and bacterial issues. Keep ammonia and nitrite at 0 with a cycled sponge filter or bottled bacteria plus testing.</p>\n<p>Calculate QT volume with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>. For new setups, complete a <a href="cycling-guide.html">fishless cycle</a> or move established media from a healthy tank.</p>'),
('ph-kh-gh-guide.html','Aquarium pH, KH and GH Explained','What pH, carbonate hardness (KH) and general hardness (GH) mean for fish tanks, and how they relate.','pH, KH and GH in Aquariums','<p><b>pH</b> measures how acidic or alkaline the water is. <b>KH</b> (carbonate hardness) buffers pH so it does not swing. <b>GH</b> (general hardness) is mainly dissolved calcium and magnesium.</p>\n<h2>Why KH matters</h2><p>Low KH lets pH crash overnight in a planted or high-bioload tank. Many community fish tolerate a range of pH if KH is stable. Chasing an exact pH with chemicals often causes more harm than leaving a stable, reasonable value.</p>\n<h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li>Test with a liquid kit; strips are rough.</li><li>Match new fish to your water when you can, instead of constantly remaking RO blends.</li><li>Driftwood and CO2 tend to lower pH; crushed coral and limestone raise KH/GH.</li></ul>\n<p>Hardness does not replace cycling: still keep ammonia and nitrite at 0. See the <a href="cycling-guide.html">cycling guide</a> and <a href="water-change.html">water change calculator</a>.</p>'),
('goldfish-tank-size.html','Goldfish Tank Size: How Many Gallons Per Fish','How big a tank goldfish need: why bowls fail, fancy vs common goldfish gallons, filtration and water-change tips.','How Big a Tank Do Goldfish Need?','''<p>Fancy goldfish usually need at least <b>20 US gallons for the first fish</b> and about 10 more gallons for each extra fancy. Common and comet goldfish get much larger and often need 40-75+ gallons each as adults, ideally in a pond or very large tank.</p>
<h2>Why goldfish need more space</h2><p>They produce a heavy bioload, grow larger than store juveniles suggest, and foul small bowls quickly. A filtered, cycled tank with frequent water changes matters more than decorations.</p>
<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Rough planning</th></tr><tr><td>Fancy (1)</td><td>~20 gal minimum, overfilter</td></tr><tr><td>Fancy (pair)</td><td>~30-40 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Common / comet</td><td>Large tank or outdoor pond; skip nano tanks</td></tr></table>
<p>Use the <a href="index.html">volume calculator</a> for real water volume after substrate, then size filtration above the usual community rule. See the <a href="filter-flow-rate.html">filter GPH guide</a> and <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a>.</p>'''),
('dechlorinator-dose.html','Aquarium Dechlorinator Dose Calculator','Dose water conditioner by tank gallons: enter volume and product strength for a starting ml estimate.','Dechlorinator Dose by Gallons','''<label>Tank or change volume (US gal) <input id="dv" type="number" value="20" step="any"></label><label>Product label (ml per gal for full dose) <input id="dm" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var g=+document.getElementById('dv').value,m=+document.getElementById('dm').value;document.getElementById('dq').textContent='About '+(g*m).toFixed(1)+' ml for '+g+' gal (follow your bottle; many brands are ~1 ml per gallon for chloramine). Dose for the water you add, or the full tank if the label says so.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="dq"></div>
<h2>Chlorine vs chloramine</h2><p>Many city supplies use chloramine. Conditioners that only neutralize free chlorine may leave ammonia behind unless the product also binds chloramine. Read the label and dose for the volume you treat.</p>
<p>Match temperature on water changes. Pair with the <a href="water-change.html">water change calculator</a> and <a href="ammonia-calculator.html">ammonia dilution tool</a>.</p>'''),
('co2-drop-checker.html','Aquarium CO2 Drop Checker & ppm Guide','What a drop checker color means for planted-tank CO2, rough ppm targets, and safety tips for fish.','CO2 Drop Checker Colors & Targets','''<p>A drop checker with 4 dKH indicator solution is a common way to eyeball dissolved CO2 in planted tanks. Color is a rough guide, not a lab reading.</p>
<table><tr><th>Color (typical)</th><th>Rough meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Blue</td><td>Low CO2</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>Often ~30 ppm target zone</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>High CO2; risk to fish</td></tr></table>
<h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li>Aim for green during the photoperiod; adjust injection slowly over days.</li><li>Watch fish behavior (gasping at surface) more than the checker alone.</li><li>Pair CO2 with enough light and fertilizer; CO2 alone will not fix algae.</li></ul>
<p>Lighting targets are in the <a href="led-lighting.html">LED lighting guide</a>. For tank volume when dosing, use the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('how-often-clean-filter.html','How Often to Clean an Aquarium Filter','When to rinse filter media, how to preserve beneficial bacteria, and signs your filter is clogged.','How Often Should You Clean Your Filter?','''<p>Rinse mechanical media when flow drops or every few weeks, in <b>old tank water</b>, not tap water. Beneficial bacteria live on media; hot or chlorinated water can crash the cycle.</p>
<h2>Rules of thumb</h2><ul><li>Never replace all media at once in a cycled tank.</li><li>Stagger sponge, floss and ceramic cleanings.</li><li>If ammonia or nitrite appear after a deep clean, do water changes and avoid more media swaps.</li></ul>
<p>Size flow with the <a href="filter-flow-rate.html">GPH guide</a>. If you reset the filter on a new setup, follow the <a href="cycling-guide.html">fishless cycling guide</a>.</p>'''),
('aquarium-stand-weight.html','Aquarium Stand Weight Limits & Floor Load Basics','How to think about stand ratings and floor load for full aquariums, with links to filled-weight estimates.','Aquarium Stands and Floor Load','''<p>Buy a stand rated for your <b>filled</b> weight, not the empty glass rating. Water alone is about 8.34 lb per US gallon; substrate, rock and the tank add more.</p>
<h2>Checklist</h2><ul><li>Level the stand; shims beat a rocking cabinet.</li><li>Spread load on joists when you can; avoid mid-span on long tanks when unsure.</li><li>Upper floors and older homes may need a qualified opinion for large tanks.</li></ul>
<p>Estimate water weight with the <a href="tank-weight.html">filled weight chart</a> and real gallons from the <a href="index.html">calculator</a>. This page is planning guidance only, not structural engineering advice.</p>'''),

('fish-tank-electricity-cost.html','Fish Tank Electricity Cost Calculator: Heater, Light & Filter','Estimate how much your aquarium costs to run per month: enter heater, light and filter watts, hours per day and your electricity price.','How Much Electricity Does a Fish Tank Use?','''<label>Heater watts <input id="eh" type="number" value="100"></label><label>Heater on-time (% of day, often 20-50%) <input id="ed" type="number" value="35"></label><label>Light watts <input id="el" type="number" value="20"></label><label>Light hours per day <input id="elh" type="number" value="8"></label><label>Filter / pump watts (runs 24 h) <input id="ef" type="number" value="10"></label><label>Electricity price per kWh <input id="ep" type="number" value="0.17" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var v=function(i){return +document.getElementById(i).value};var kwh=(v('eh')*24*v('ed')/100+v('el')*v('elh')+v('ef')*24)/1000;document.getElementById('eo').textContent='About '+kwh.toFixed(2)+' kWh per day, '+(kwh*30).toFixed(0)+' kWh per month, roughly '+(kwh*30*v('ep')).toFixed(2)+' per month at your price.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="eo"></div>
<h2>How the estimate works</h2><p>Energy (kWh) = watts x hours / 1000. Filters and air pumps usually run around the clock. Lights run for the photoperiod you set. A heater only draws power while its thermostat is on, so the share of the day it runs depends on how much warmer the tank is than the room, the lid, and the tank size.</p>
<h2>Ways to cut the cost</h2><ul><li>Use a lid or cover glass to reduce heat loss and evaporation.</li><li>Keep the tank away from drafts, windows and exterior walls.</li><li>Switch older fluorescent fixtures to LED.</li><li>Size the heater sensibly with the <a href="heater-size.html">heater guide</a>; an oversized heater does not use more energy on its own, but a room that is much colder than the tank does.</li></ul>
<p>Check your own electricity bill for the real price per kWh; the default here is only an example value.</p>'''),
('aquarium-temperature-guide.html','Aquarium Temperature Guide: Tropical, Goldfish & Coldwater Tanks','Typical aquarium temperature ranges for tropical community fish, goldfish and coldwater tanks, with a Fahrenheit to Celsius converter.','What Temperature Should a Fish Tank Be?','''<label>Temperature <input id="tv" type="number" value="78" step="any"></label><label>From <select id="tu"><option value="f">Fahrenheit</option><option value="c">Celsius</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var t=+document.getElementById('tv').value,u=document.getElementById('tu').value;document.getElementById('to').textContent=u==='f'?t+' F = '+((t-32)*5/9).toFixed(1)+' C':t+' C = '+(t*9/5+32).toFixed(1)+' F'">Convert</button><div class="out" id="to"></div>
<p>Most tropical community fish are kept around <b>75-80 F (24-27 C)</b>. Fancy goldfish and other coldwater species are commonly kept cooler, often in the mid 60s to low 70s F (about 18-22 C), without a heater in a normally heated home. Check the needs of each species you keep.</p>
<h2>Common hobby ranges</h2><table><tr><th>Tank type</th><th>Typical range</th></tr><tr><td>Tropical community</td><td>75-80 F (24-27 C)</td></tr><tr><td>Betta</td><td>76-82 F (24-28 C)</td></tr><tr><td>Goldfish / coldwater</td><td>~65-72 F (18-22 C)</td></tr><tr><td>Discus</td><td>Warmer, often 82-86 F (28-30 C)</td></tr></table>
<h2>Stability matters most</h2><p>Sudden swings stress fish more than a degree or two off target. Match the temperature of new water during water changes and use a separate thermometer to check the heater's dial. See the <a href="heater-size.html">heater size guide</a> and the <a href="betta-tank-size.html">betta tank guide</a>.</p>'''),
('shrimp-tank-size.html','Shrimp Tank Size: How Many Shrimp per Gallon','What size tank dwarf shrimp like cherry shrimp need, common stocking guidance per gallon, and setup tips for a stable shrimp tank.','How Big a Tank Do Shrimp Need?','''<p>Dwarf shrimp such as cherry shrimp are often kept in tanks from about <b>5 US gallons</b> upward. Larger tanks are more stable, which matters more for shrimp than for many fish. A common hobby rule of thumb is up to roughly 5-10 dwarf shrimp per gallon in a mature, planted tank, starting with a smaller group and letting them breed.</p>
<h2>Setup tips</h2><ul><li>Use a sponge filter or cover the filter intake so baby shrimp are not pulled in.</li><li>Add shrimp only to a fully <a href="cycling-guide.html">cycled tank</a>; mature tanks with biofilm do best.</li><li>Avoid copper-based medications and fertilizers, which are commonly reported as harmful to invertebrates.</li><li>Keep temperature and hardness stable; read the <a href="ph-kh-gh-guide.html">pH, KH and GH guide</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>Tank mates</h2><p>Many fish will eat shrimp, especially babies. Shrimp-only tanks or very small, peaceful tank mates give the best results.</p>
<p>Work out real water volume with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a> and check how much substrate you need in the <a href="substrate-guide.html">substrate guide</a>.</p>'''),
('aquarium-evaporation.html','Aquarium Evaporation & Top-Off Calculator','Estimate how much water your aquarium loses to evaporation and how much top-off water to add, plus why you top off with fresh water.','Aquarium Evaporation and Top-Off','''<label>Water level drop (inches) <input id="vd" type="number" value="0.5" step="any"></label><label>Tank length (in) <input id="vl" type="number" value="30" step="any"></label><label>Tank width (in) <input id="vw" type="number" value="12" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var g=(+document.getElementById('vd').value)*(+document.getElementById('vl').value)*(+document.getElementById('vw').value)/231;document.getElementById('vo').textContent='About '+g.toFixed(2)+' US gal ('+(g*3.785).toFixed(1)+' L) to top off.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="vo"></div>
<p>Top-off volume = water level drop x length x width (inches) / 231 for US gallons. Measure the drop from your usual fill line.</p>
<h2>Top off with fresh water</h2><p>Only water evaporates; dissolved minerals and salt stay behind. That is why reef and saltwater tanks are topped off with fresh RO/DI water, not saltwater. In freshwater tanks, dechlorinate tap top-off water (see the <a href="dechlorinator-dose.html">dechlorinator dose calculator</a>).</p>
<h2>Why evaporation varies</h2><p>Open tops, warm water, strong surface agitation and dry room air all increase evaporation. A lid cuts it noticeably and also saves heater energy (see the <a href="fish-tank-electricity-cost.html">electricity cost calculator</a>).</p>'''),
('cylinder-tank-volume.html','Cylinder Aquarium Volume Calculator (Gallons & Liters)','Calculate the volume of a round or cylinder fish tank from diameter and height in inches or centimeters.','Cylinder Tank Volume Calculator','''<label>Inside diameter <input id="cd" type="number" value="12" step="any"></label><label>Water height <input id="ch" type="number" value="24" step="any"></label><label>Units <select id="cu"><option value="in">inches</option><option value="cm">cm</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var d=+document.getElementById('cd').value,h=+document.getElementById('ch').value,u=document.getElementById('cu').value;var v=Math.PI*(d/2)*(d/2)*h;var gal=u==='in'?v/231:v/3785.41;document.getElementById('co').textContent='About '+gal.toFixed(1)+' US gal ('+(gal*3.785).toFixed(1)+' L).'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="co"></div>
<h2>Formula</h2><p>Volume = pi x radius squared x height. In inches, divide cubic inches by 231 for US gallons. In centimeters, divide cubic cm by 1000 for liters. Example: a 12 in diameter tank filled to 24 in holds about 11.8 US gallons.</p>
<h2>Half-cylinder and other shapes</h2><p>For a half-cylinder (flat back), halve the result. Rectangular, bow-front and hexagon formulas are in the <a href="tank-volume.html">tank volume guide</a>, and the main <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a> handles rectangular tanks.</p>'''),
('turtle-tank-size.html','Turtle Tank Size: How Many Gallons Does a Turtle Need?','Common turtle tank size guidance: the 10 gallons per inch of shell rule, sizes for red-eared sliders and musk turtles, plus basking and filter tips.','What Size Tank Does a Turtle Need?','''<label>Shell length (inches) <input id="ts" type="number" value="6" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var s=+document.getElementById('ts').value;document.getElementById('tto').textContent='Rule of thumb: about '+Math.round(s*10)+' US gallons ('+Math.round(s*10*3.785)+' L) of tank for one aquatic turtle with a '+s+' inch shell.'">Estimate</button><div class="out" id="tto"></div>
<p>A widely repeated hobby rule for aquatic turtles is <b>about 10 US gallons of tank per inch of shell length</b> for one turtle, with more space for each extra turtle. Sliders and cooters grow large, so many keepers plan for the adult size rather than the hatchling.</p>
<h2>Typical adult sizes (approximate)</h2><table><tr><th>Species</th><th>Adult shell</th><th>Rule-of-thumb tank</th></tr><tr><td>Red-eared slider (male)</td><td>~7-9 in</td><td>~75-90 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Red-eared slider (female)</td><td>~10-12 in</td><td>~100-125 gal or a pond</td></tr><tr><td>Musk / mud turtle</td><td>~4-5 in</td><td>~40-50 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Painted turtle</td><td>~5-7 in</td><td>~55-75 gal</td></tr></table><p>Sizes vary by individual and source; check a species-specific care guide.</p>
<h2>Setup tips</h2><ul><li>Provide a dry basking area with a heat lamp and UVB lighting.</li><li>Turtles are messy; many keepers use a filter rated for two to three times the water volume.</li><li>Water depth of at least about 1.5 to 2 times the shell length lets them turn over safely.</li></ul>
<p>Check how much a full tank weighs with the <a href="tank-weight.html">tank weight chart</a> and compare standard sizes in the <a href="tank-sizes.html">aquarium sizes chart</a>.</p>'''),
('axolotl-tank-size.html','Axolotl Tank Size & Setup: Gallons, Temperature and Substrate','What size tank an axolotl needs, the cool water temperatures they are commonly kept at, and substrate and filter tips.','What Size Tank Does an Axolotl Need?','''<p>A common hobby recommendation is a <b>20 gallon long</b> tank (30 x 12 in footprint) as a minimum for one adult axolotl, with roughly another 10 gallons for each additional axolotl. Footprint matters more than height because axolotls live on the bottom.</p>
<h2>Temperature</h2><p>Axolotls are coldwater animals. They are commonly kept around <b>60-64 F (16-18 C)</b>, and many sources warn that sustained temperatures above about 72 F (22 C) cause stress. They generally do not need a heater; in warm rooms keepers use fans or chillers. Convert units with the <a href="aquarium-temperature-guide.html">temperature guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Substrate and flow</h2><ul><li>Use bare bottom or fine sand; gravel small enough to swallow is a commonly cited impaction risk.</li><li>Axolotls prefer gentle flow, so a sponge filter or a baffled outlet is popular.</li><li>Cycle the tank fully first (see the <a href="cycling-guide.html">fishless cycling guide</a>).</li></ul>
<p>Work out how much sand you need with the <a href="substrate-guide.html">substrate guide</a> and confirm your real water volume with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('aquarium-light-hours.html','How Long to Leave Aquarium Lights On: Photoperiod Guide','How many hours a day to run aquarium lights for fish-only and planted tanks, how to handle algae, and a light cost estimate.','How Many Hours Should Aquarium Lights Be On?','''<p>Common hobby guidance is about <b>6-8 hours a day</b> for most fish-only and planted tanks, and up to around 10 hours for well-balanced planted setups. Fish do not need light at night, and a regular day-night cycle is generally recommended.</p>
<h2>Typical photoperiods</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Hours per day (rule of thumb)</th></tr><tr><td>Fish-only</td><td>6-8</td></tr><tr><td>Low-tech planted</td><td>6-8</td></tr><tr><td>High-light planted with CO2</td><td>7-10</td></tr><tr><td>New tank or algae outbreak</td><td>Start at ~6 and increase slowly</td></tr></table>
<h2>Algae and timers</h2><ul><li>Too many hours of strong light is a common cause of algae; shorten the photoperiod before reaching for chemicals.</li><li>A simple outlet timer keeps the schedule consistent.</li><li>Some keepers split the day with a mid-day break (a "siesta"); results are mixed, so treat it as optional.</li></ul>
<p>See the <a href="led-lighting.html">LED lighting guide</a> and estimate running costs with the <a href="fish-tank-electricity-cost.html">fish tank electricity cost calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('sump-size.html','Aquarium Sump Size Calculator: How Big Should a Sump Be?','How to size an aquarium sump: the common 10-25% of display volume rule, how to handle power-outage drain-back, and a quick calculator.','How Big Should Your Sump Be?','''<label>Display tank volume (US gal) <input id="sv" type="number" value="75"></label><button type="button" onclick="var v=+document.getElementById('sv').value;document.getElementById('so').textContent='Rule of thumb: about '+Math.round(v*0.2)+'-'+Math.round(v*0.3)+' US gallons of sump ('+Math.round(v*0.2*3.785)+'-'+Math.round(v*0.3*3.785)+' L).'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="so"></div>
<p>A common guideline is a sump of roughly <b>20-30% of the display tank volume</b>, with bigger usually better as long as it fits in the stand. The calculator above uses that range.</p>
<h2>Plan for drain-back</h2><p>When the return pump stops, water from the display drains down until it falls below the overflow, and return lines can siphon back. The sump must have enough empty space to hold that water without overflowing. Test it by switching the pump off with the sump at its normal running level.</p>
<h2>Drain-back volume estimate</h2><p>Drain-back from the display = water height above the overflow teeth (in) x display length x width (in) / 231 for US gallons. Add the volume of the plumbing that can siphon back.</p>
<h2>Return pump flow</h2><p>Many keepers aim for a sump turnover of around 3-10 times the display volume per hour, depending on the setup. See the <a href="filter-flow-rate.html">filter flow rate guide</a> and check the stand load with the <a href="aquarium-stand-weight.html">stand weight guide</a>.</p>'''),
('salinity-converter.html','Salinity Converter: PPT to Specific Gravity for Saltwater Aquariums','Convert saltwater aquarium salinity between parts per thousand (ppt) and specific gravity, with common reef and fish-only targets.','Salinity: PPT to Specific Gravity Converter','''<label>Value <input id="sav" type="number" value="35" step="any"></label><label>From <select id="sau"><option value="ppt">ppt</option><option value="sg">specific gravity</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var x=+document.getElementById('sav').value,u=document.getElementById('sau').value;document.getElementById('sao').textContent=u==='ppt'?x+' ppt is about SG '+(1+x*0.000754).toFixed(4):'SG '+x+' is about '+((x-1)/0.000754).toFixed(1)+' ppt'">Convert</button><div class="out" id="sao"></div>
<p>This uses a linear approximation (SG of about 1 + 0.000754 x ppt) that is close for normal aquarium salinities at around 77 F (25 C). It is a quick estimate, not a laboratory conversion; temperature-compensated refractometers and meters are more precise.</p>
<h2>Common targets</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Typical salinity</th></tr><tr><td>Reef tank</td><td>~35 ppt (SG ~1.025-1.026)</td></tr><tr><td>Fish-only</td><td>Often slightly lower, ~1.020-1.025</td></tr><tr><td>Natural seawater</td><td>~35 ppt</td></tr></table>
<h2>Keep it stable</h2><p>Evaporation raises salinity because salt stays behind, so top off with fresh RO/DI water. Estimate top-off volume with the <a href="aquarium-evaporation.html">evaporation calculator</a> and freshwater salt dosing with the <a href="aquarium-salt-calculator.html">aquarium salt calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('oscar-tank-size.html','Oscar Fish Tank Size: How Many Gallons for One or Two Oscars?','How big a tank an oscar fish needs, with common minimums for one adult and a pair, filtration tips and tank mates.','What Size Tank Does an Oscar Need?','''<p>Oscars grow fast and commonly reach around <b>10-12 inches</b> as adults. A widely cited hobby minimum is about <b>75 US gallons for one adult oscar</b>, with roughly <b>125 gallons or more for a pair</b>. A long footprint (at least about 18 in front to back) gives them room to turn.</p>
<h2>Rule-of-thumb sizes</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Common minimum</th></tr><tr><td>One juvenile (under 4 in)</td><td>~40 gal, temporary only</td></tr><tr><td>One adult</td><td>~75 gal</td></tr><tr><td>A pair</td><td>~125 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Oscar plus large tank mates</td><td>~150 gal or more</td></tr></table>
<h2>Filtration and maintenance</h2><ul><li>Oscars are messy eaters, so many keepers run filtration rated for well over the tank volume (see the <a href="filter-flow-rate.html">filter flow rate guide</a>).</li><li>Large weekly water changes help keep nitrate down; check yours with the <a href="nitrate-calculator.html">nitrate calculator</a>.</li><li>Keep a secure lid, since oscars can jump.</li><li>They are commonly kept around 74-80 F (23-27 C); size a heater with the <a href="heater-size.html">heater calculator</a>.</li></ul>
<p>A full 75 gallon tank is heavy. Check the load with the <a href="tank-weight.html">tank weight chart</a> and the <a href="aquarium-stand-weight.html">stand weight guide</a>.</p>'''),
('guppy-tank-size.html','Guppy Tank Size: How Many Guppies in a 5, 10 or 20 Gallon Tank?','How big a tank guppies need, how many guppies fit in common tank sizes, male to female ratios and breeding tips.','What Size Tank Do Guppies Need?','''<label>Tank volume (US gal) <input id="gv" type="number" value="10" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var v=+document.getElementById('gv').value;var n=Math.floor(v/2);document.getElementById('go').textContent=(v<5?'Under 5 gallons is generally considered too small for guppies. ':'')+'Rough guide: about '+Math.max(0,Math.floor(n*0.6))+'-'+Math.max(0,n)+' adult guppies, before counting fry or other fish.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="go"></div>
<p>Adult guppies are small (about 1.5-2.5 in), but they are active and breed quickly. A common recommendation is a <b>10 gallon tank as a minimum</b> for a small group, with 20 gallons giving far more stability. The calculator uses a cautious rule of roughly one guppy per 2 gallons as an upper bound, and fewer if you want room for fry.</p>
<h2>Male to female ratio</h2><p>Males chase females constantly. Keepers often suggest <b>one male to two or three females</b>, or an all-male tank if you do not want fry.</p>
<h2>Water and care</h2><ul><li>Guppies are commonly kept around 72-82 F (22-28 C).</li><li>They tolerate a fairly wide pH range and many do well in moderately hard water (see the <a href="ph-kh-gh-guide.html">pH, KH and GH guide</a>).</li><li>A mixed-sex group can multiply quickly, so plan for fry or rehoming.</li></ul>
<p>For a whole community, compare with the <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a> and confirm your real water volume with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('neon-tetra-tank-size.html','Neon Tetra Tank Size: How Many Neon Tetras per Gallon?','How big a tank neon tetras need, how large a school to keep, water temperature and tips for adding them to a new tank.','What Size Tank Do Neon Tetras Need?','''<p>Neon tetras are small (about 1.5 in) schooling fish. A common recommendation is a <b>school of at least 6, and ideally 8-10 or more</b>, in a tank of <b>at least 10 US gallons</b>. A 20 gallon tank makes a larger school easier and keeps water conditions more stable.</p>
<h2>School size by tank (rule of thumb)</h2><table><tr><th>Tank</th><th>Neons, if they are the main fish</th></tr><tr><td>10 gal</td><td>~6-8</td></tr><tr><td>20 gal</td><td>~10-15</td></tr><tr><td>29-30 gal</td><td>~15-20</td></tr></table><p>Reduce these numbers if you keep other fish in the same tank.</p>
<h2>Water and care</h2><ul><li>Neons are commonly kept around 70-81 F (21-27 C).</li><li>Many keepers report they do best in soft, slightly acidic water; see the <a href="ph-kh-gh-guide.html">pH, KH and GH guide</a>.</li><li>They are often described as sensitive to new tanks, so add them only after the tank is fully cycled (see the <a href="cycling-guide.html">cycling guide</a>).</li><li>Quarantine new fish first (see the <a href="quarantine-tank-size.html">quarantine tank guide</a>).</li></ul>
<p>Plan a full community with the <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a>.</p>'''),
('aquarium-surface-area.html','Aquarium Surface Area Calculator (Square Inches, Feet & cm)','Calculate the water surface area of your fish tank in square inches, square feet and square centimeters, and why surface area matters for oxygen.','Aquarium Surface Area Calculator','''<label>Inside length <input id="al" type="number" value="24" step="any"></label><label>Inside width <input id="aw" type="number" value="12" step="any"></label><label>Units <select id="au"><option value="in">inches</option><option value="cm">cm</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var l=+document.getElementById('al').value,w=+document.getElementById('aw').value,u=document.getElementById('au').value;var si=u=='in'?l*w:l*w/6.4516;document.getElementById('ao').textContent='Surface area: '+Math.round(si)+' sq in = '+(si/144).toFixed(2)+' sq ft = '+Math.round(si*6.4516)+' sq cm.'">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="ao"></div>
<p>For a rectangular tank, surface area is simply the inside <b>length x width</b>. Height does not change it, which is why a long, shallow tank has more surface than a tall tank of the same volume.</p>
<h2>Why surface area matters</h2><p>Oxygen enters and carbon dioxide leaves the water at the surface. Two tanks with the same gallons can support different amounts of fish if one has much more surface. Surface agitation from a filter outlet or air stone increases gas exchange further.</p>
<h2>The old surface area rule</h2><p>An older hobby rule suggests about 1 inch of fish per 12 square inches of surface for tropical fish, and far less for coldwater fish such as goldfish. Like the inch-per-gallon rule, it ignores adult size, body shape and filtration, so treat it as a rough check only. The <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a> covers better approaches.</p>
<p>Other shapes: see the <a href="tank-volume.html">tank volume guide</a> and the <a href="cylinder-tank-volume.html">cylinder tank calculator</a> (a cylinder's surface is pi x radius squared).</p>'''),
('how-much-to-feed-fish.html','How Much and How Often to Feed Aquarium Fish','How much to feed aquarium fish, how often, signs of overfeeding, and what to do when you go on vacation.','How Much Should You Feed Your Fish?','''<p>A common rule is to feed <b>only what your fish finish in about 1-2 minutes, once or twice a day</b>. Most aquarium problems with feeding come from giving too much, not too little.</p>
<h2>Why overfeeding is a problem</h2><p>Uneaten food breaks down into ammonia, which feeds algae and can harm fish. If you see food on the substrate after a few minutes, feed less. Check what an ammonia reading means with the <a href="ammonia-calculator.html">ammonia calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Typical schedules (rule of thumb)</h2><table><tr><th>Fish</th><th>Common schedule</th></tr><tr><td>Most adult community fish</td><td>Once or twice a day, small portions</td></tr><tr><td>Fry and juveniles</td><td>Several small feedings a day</td></tr><tr><td>Large predatory fish (e.g. oscars)</td><td>Often once a day or every other day as adults</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom feeders</td><td>Sinking food so it reaches them</td></tr></table>
<h2>Fasting and vacations</h2><ul><li>Many keepers skip feeding one day a week for adult fish.</li><li>Healthy adult fish can usually go a few days without food; for longer trips, an automatic feeder you have tested beforehand is a common choice.</li><li>Avoid dropping in large "vacation blocks" in small tanks, as they can cloud the water.</li></ul>
<p>Overfeeding also raises nitrate between water changes; plan changes with the <a href="water-change.html">water change calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('water-hardness-converter.html','Water Hardness Converter: dGH, dKH and ppm for Aquariums','Convert aquarium water hardness between degrees (dGH or dKH) and ppm (mg/L as CaCO3), with common ranges for soft and hard water fish.','Aquarium Water Hardness Converter','''<label>Value <input id="hv" type="number" value="6" step="any"></label><label>From <select id="hu"><option value="deg">degrees (dGH or dKH)</option><option value="ppm">ppm (mg/L as CaCO3)</option></select></label><button type="button" onclick="var x=+document.getElementById('hv').value,u=document.getElementById('hu').value;document.getElementById('ho').textContent=u==='deg'?x+' degrees = about '+(x*17.848).toFixed(0)+' ppm':x+' ppm = about '+(x/17.848).toFixed(1)+' degrees'">Convert</button><div class="out" id="ho"></div>
<p>Test kits report hardness either in <b>German degrees</b> (dGH for general hardness, dKH for carbonate hardness) or in <b>ppm</b>, meaning milligrams per liter expressed as calcium carbonate. The conversion is the same for both: <b>1 degree = 17.848 ppm</b>. Many test kit instructions round this to 17.9.</p>
<h2>Rough hardness bands (GH)</h2><table><tr><th>Degrees</th><th>ppm (approx.)</th><th>Often described as</th></tr><tr><td>0-4</td><td>0-70</td><td>Very soft</td></tr><tr><td>4-8</td><td>70-140</td><td>Soft</td></tr><tr><td>8-12</td><td>140-210</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>12-18</td><td>210-320</td><td>Fairly hard</td></tr><tr><td>18+</td><td>320+</td><td>Hard</td></tr></table><p>Labels for these bands vary between sources, so treat them as a guide.</p>
<h2>Which number matters</h2><p>GH is mostly calcium and magnesium and matters for fish, shrimp and snail health. KH buffers pH and keeps it from crashing. The <a href="ph-kh-gh-guide.html">pH, KH and GH guide</a> explains how they fit together, and the <a href="co2-calculator.html">CO2 calculator</a> uses KH to estimate dissolved CO2.</p>'''),
('co2-calculator.html','Aquarium CO2 Calculator from pH and KH','Estimate dissolved CO2 in a planted aquarium from pH and KH using the standard pH/KH formula, with its limits explained.','Aquarium CO2 Calculator (pH and KH)','''<label>pH <input id="qp" type="number" value="6.8" step="any"></label><label>KH (dKH) <input id="qk" type="number" value="4" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var p=+document.getElementById('qp').value,k=+document.getElementById('qk').value;var c=3*k*Math.pow(10,7-p);document.getElementById('qo').textContent='Estimated CO2: about '+c.toFixed(1)+' ppm'+(c>40?' (high; watch fish closely)':c<15?' (low for a CO2-injected planted tank)':'')">Calculate</button><div class="out" id="qo"></div>
<h2>The formula</h2><p>The common hobby estimate is <b>CO2 (ppm) = 3 x KH (dKH) x 10^(7 - pH)</b>. Example: KH 4 and pH 6.8 gives 3 x 4 x 1.58, or about 19 ppm. Many planted tank keepers aim for around 30 ppm during the light period.</p>
<h2>Why it can be wrong</h2><p>The formula assumes carbonate is the only buffer in the water. Driftwood tannins, aquasoils, phosphate buffers and pH-adjusting products all throw it off, sometimes by a lot. That is why many keepers prefer a <a href="co2-drop-checker.html">drop checker</a> or measure the pH drop from fully degassed water to lights-on.</p>
<h2>Safety</h2><p>Fish gasping at the surface is a sign to reduce CO2 and increase surface agitation right away. Convert kit readings with the <a href="water-hardness-converter.html">water hardness converter</a> and match light levels with the <a href="led-lighting.html">LED lighting guide</a>.</p>'''),
('angelfish-tank-size.html','Angelfish Tank Size: How Many Gallons for Freshwater Angelfish?','How big a tank freshwater angelfish need, why tank height matters, common sizes for a pair or a group, and water temperature.','What Size Tank Do Angelfish Need?','''<p>Freshwater angelfish can reach around <b>6 inches long and 8 inches tall</b> including fins. A common hobby recommendation is a tank of <b>at least 29-30 US gallons for a single angelfish or a pair</b>, and <b>55 gallons or more for a small group</b>. Height matters: many keepers look for tanks at least about 18 inches tall.</p>
<h2>Rule-of-thumb sizes</h2><table><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Common minimum</th></tr><tr><td>One or a pair</td><td>~29-30 gal, tall</td></tr><tr><td>Group of 4-6 juveniles</td><td>~55 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Group in a mixed community</td><td>~75 gal or more</td></tr></table><p>Groups often pair off and become territorial as they mature, so plan for extra space or rehoming.</p>
<h2>Care notes</h2><ul><li>Angelfish are commonly kept around 76-82 F (24-28 C); size the heater with the <a href="heater-size.html">heater guide</a>.</li><li>They may eat very small fish such as adult neon tetras as they grow (see the <a href="neon-tetra-tank-size.html">neon tetra guide</a>).</li><li>Gentle to moderate flow and tall plants or decor suit them.</li></ul>
<p>Compare tank heights in the <a href="tank-sizes.html">aquarium sizes chart</a> and confirm real volume with the <a href="index.html">aquarium calculator</a>.</p>'''),
('corydoras-tank-size.html','Corydoras Tank Size: How Many Cory Catfish per Gallon?','How big a tank cory catfish need, why they should be kept in groups, the best substrate and a quick group-size estimate.','What Size Tank Do Corydoras Need?','''<label>Tank footprint length (in) <input id="yl" type="number" value="30" step="any"></label><button type="button" onclick="var l=+document.getElementById('yl').value;document.getElementById('yo').textContent=l<24?'Under about 24 in long is tight for most corydoras; consider pygmy species or a longer tank.':'A tank this long can usually hold a group of '+(l<30?'6':'6-10')+' small corydoras, depending on species and other fish.'">Estimate</button><div class="out" id="yo"></div>
<p>Corydoras are small, peaceful bottom dwellers that live in groups. A common recommendation is a group of <b>at least 6 of the same species</b> in a tank of <b>at least 20 US gallons</b>, ideally a 20 long (30 x 12 in) because they use the bottom, not the height. Pygmy and dwarf species can work in smaller tanks.</p>
<h2>Substrate</h2><p>Corys sift through the bottom for food. Many keepers prefer smooth sand or fine, rounded gravel so their barbels are not damaged. Work out how much to buy with the <a href="substrate-guide.html">substrate guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Care notes</h2><ul><li>Many common species are kept around 72-78 F (22-26 C); check your species.</li><li>Use sinking food so it reaches them (see <a href="how-much-to-feed-fish.html">how much to feed fish</a>).</li><li>They are often reported as sensitive to salt and some medications, so check labels before treating (see the <a href="aquarium-salt-calculator.html">salt guide</a>).</li></ul>
<p>Plan the rest of the community with the <a href="stocking-guide.html">stocking guide</a>.</p>'''),
('pleco-tank-size.html','Pleco Tank Size: Bristlenose vs Common Pleco Gallons','How big a tank plecos need: why a common pleco outgrows most aquariums, bristlenose pleco sizes, driftwood and filtration tips.','What Size Tank Does a Pleco Need?','''<p>"Pleco" covers many species with very different adult sizes. The <b>bristlenose pleco</b> usually stays around 4-5 inches and is commonly kept in tanks from about <b>20-30 US gallons</b>. The <b>common pleco</b> sold as a small juvenile can grow to around 12-18 inches or more and often needs <b>125 gallons or more</b>, or a pond.</p>
<h2>Rough sizes by type</h2><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Adult length (approx.)</th><th>Common minimum tank</th></tr><tr><td>Bristlenose</td><td>4-5 in</td><td>~20-30 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Clown pleco</td><td>3.5-4 in</td><td>~20-30 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Rubber lip</td><td>5-7 in</td><td>~30-40 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Common / sailfin</td><td>12-18+ in</td><td>~125 gal or more</td></tr></table><p>Sizes vary by source and individual; check the species before buying.</p>
<h2>Care notes</h2><ul><li>Plecos produce a lot of waste, so size filtration generously (see the <a href="filter-flow-rate.html">filter flow rate guide</a>).</li><li>Many species rasp on driftwood, and some keepers consider it an important part of their diet.</li><li>Feed sinking wafers and vegetables rather than relying on algae alone.</li></ul>
<p>A tank big enough for a common pleco is heavy; check the <a href="tank-weight.html">tank weight chart</a>.</p>'''),
]
